#!/usr/bin/env bash
# lane-improve: away-improve mode, the owner's switch for it (core/improve.py; docs/superpowers/specs/
# 2026-11-23-away-improve-design.md). Each chosen project's coordinator keeps improving its product on its one away
# branch, cycle after cycle, spending the owner's usage. It is separate from presence: `lane-away` says the owner is away
# (limits, check-ins); this says what the machine may spend its time on. Neither starts the other.
#
#   lane-improve on [--until HH:MM|--hours N|--until-back] [--limits "<text>"] [--deny-paths "<glob>,..."]
#                   [--project <name>]... [--unmetered] [--stop-at <weekly %>] [--max-items N] [--continue] [--yes]
#       Switches the mode on, and it is the ONLY way it turns on: plain `lane-away on`, night, "goodnight", a changed budget
#       never do, and an agent runs it only on the owner's own explicit request for the improve (away-improve) mode.
#       Prints what it turned on, and without a terminal refuses unless --yes. End: a time, or until you run
#       `lane-improve off` (the default). --project (repeatable) limits it to those coordinators (default: all);
#       --unmetered marks a machine with no usage limits. Each project gets ONE away branch, ever: a new away/<date> cut
#       from its integration branch (the base of its recent merged PRs, else origin's default). A project that already has
#       one is refused: --continue carries on with it (it takes no argument; a project with none gets one cut, so a new
#       project can join; two or more are refused and listed), or the owner adopts it (merges it to the integration branch)
#       or drops it (deletes it) first. A `--continue` re-run while on keeps what is not given again; without --project it keeps the projects as they were (no branch cut). Nothing is merged.
#       Start commit: a new branch is the integration head plus one commit, "away: start <date>", adding the first AWAY-LOG.md (the
#       template: header, limits, which projects, an empty summary); a branch that --continue carries on gets none. Without a git
#       identity (or with a log already on the integration branch) it is cut from the head alone, with a warning.
#       Draft PR: for each project whose branch it cuts or continues it opens the draft summary PR away/<date> -> the integration
#       branch (title "away: <date> (<project>)", body = AWAY-LOG.md's summary) and records its number in improve.json; when gh
#       cannot (or the start commit could not be made), away-merge opens it after the first merge (a warning says so).
#       Caps, both off unless given, both ending a project's cycles like the end time (the running lane finishes and merges,
#       then it wraps up and runs `drained`; the mode stays on for the others): --max-items N: no new cycle after N away-merges
#       in this period (away-merge's own record); --stop-at <weekly %>: none once `budget-state --project` gives weekly at or
#       above it (metered only: refused with --unmetered, or at or above the project's ceiling). A --continue re-run changes
#       them only when given; `none` drops one. `status` shows "items 4/10" and "weekly 66/70" per project. --max-items is
#       also a hard stop in away-merge (the Nth merge is the last); a wrapped-up project resumes only when named again with
#       its cap raised (`on --continue --project <name> --max-items <more>`). The period's record (improve-period.json) fails
#       closed if it is lost or unreadable (merges refused, `status` says why): `on --continue` makes it again.
#   lane-improve off
#       Ends the mode. What was running drains: no new cycle, the last PRs can still merge through away-merge, the deny
#       rules stay until each coordinator has wrapped up.
#   lane-improve drained <project>... | --all
#       A coordinator's wrap-up is done (last PR merged, draft summary PR brought up to date): its deny rules come out and the
#       drain clears. While the mode is still on it takes only a project whose cap is reached (nothing more merges for it).
#   lane-improve rest <project>
#       A coordinator's note that nothing within the limits is left: the idle nudges stop for that project for 12 h (or
#       until the mode is switched on or off again), then one nudge looks for new work. It only quiets; it starts nothing.
#   lane-improve pr <project>
#       The project's draft summary PR (away/<date> -> its integration branch, titled "away: <date> (<project>)"): opened at
#       `on` and recorded in improve.json, its body refreshed from AWAY-LOG.md's summary by every away-merge. This opens it when
#       it is missing (gh could not at `on`), refreshes it, and
#       prints the link. It stays a draft and is never merged by an agent; only the owner merges it.
#   lane-improve status
#       off, on (what runs, its limits, its projects and branches), or draining (what is left to wrap up).
# Exit: 0, 1 refused or nothing to do, 2 usage.
set -u
self=$(readlink -f "$0")
case ${1:-} in
  on | off | drained | status | rest | pr) exec python3 "${self%/*}/../improve.py" "$@" ;;
  -h | --help) sed -n '2,/^set -u/{/^set -u/d;s/^# \{0,1\}//;p}' "$self" ;;
  *) echo "usage: lane-improve on [options]|off|drained <project>|rest <project>|pr <project>|status" >&2; exit 2 ;;
esac
